// The quiet face of a kind collision, and the reason it is worth a fixture.
//
// A user `enum Allocator` does not remove the prelude STRUCT of that name - the
// reference drops prelude declarations only within their own kind - so
// `*const Allocator` here is the struct. Nothing says `match`, nothing reads a
// field, nothing fails: with the binding wrong, BOTH compilers accepted this
// program and the parameter simply meant two different types. Only the emitted
// C said so, which is why parity is the assertion and exit status would have
// been silent throughout.
//
// The file was parked for one layer longer than the binding itself. Getting the
// binding right left the struct with no declaration in the output: it is named
// in a signature and never built, and only built types were reaching the list
// of aggregates to emit. C accepts that file with a warning and gives the
// parameter a type of its own, distinct from every real Allocator - the same
// wrong meaning, one layer down. Naming the type is now enough to declare it.

enum Allocator { A | B }

fn takes(x: *const Allocator) { println(1); }

fn main() { println(1); }
